plaso.tsv: what plaso (the log2timeline/plaso:20260720 Docker image) reads
from the logs in tests/fixtures/plaso/ (santa.log uncompressed), written by
plaso.py from psort's JSON lines; one line per event: the file, the time
(ISO 8601; local times as plaso stores them, as if UTC), plaso's name for
it, the parser, and every value plaso read (see plaso.py). Written from
plaso's output, not from this crate.

The year-less logs (Snort without a year, glog, securityd, the application
firewall) are dated by plaso from the files' timestamps: they were run with
their modification time set to 2024-06-01T00:00:00Z, which tests/plaso.rs
passes as Context::modified.

Made on a Debian host with Docker, the logs in /tmp/oracle/in, from the
crate root:

  touch -d "2024-06-01 00:00:00 UTC" /tmp/oracle/in/*
  sudo docker run --rm -v /tmp/oracle:/data log2timeline/plaso:20260720 \
    log2timeline --status_view none -q \
    --parsers 'text/vsftpd,text/postgresql,text/popularity_contest,text/snort_fastlog,text/googlelog,text/santa,text/mac_securityd,text/mac_appfirewall_log' \
    --storage-file /data/out.plaso /data/in
  sudo docker run --rm -v /tmp/oracle:/data log2timeline/plaso:20260720 \
    psort --status_view none -q -a -o json_line -w /data/out.jsonl /data/out.plaso
  python3 -I tests/oracle/plaso.py /tmp/oracle/out.jsonl > tests/oracle/plaso.tsv

psort's -a keeps the events it would otherwise drop as duplicates (7 of
358 here: lines logged twice, such as popularity-contest's `at`), so that
every event is compared.
